    "['mpls ldp autoconfig area [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface all area 0', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ldp interface all area 0\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about which interfaces should run LDP (similar to the OSPF area 0 network statements in the Cisco config):\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nThe first version enables LDP on all interfaces in area 0, while the second version specifically enables it just on the GigabitEthernet1/0/0 interface (ge-1/0/0.0 in JunOS) which is in area 0. The first option more closely matches the \"autoconfig\" behavior in Cisco.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface all area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/0.0 area 0', 'parent_command': ['set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'interface [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set protocols ospf area [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ldp interface all area 0\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you want to be more specific about which interfaces should run LDP (similar to the OSPF area 0 network statements in the Cisco config):\\n##\\nset protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/0.0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nThe first version enables LDP on all interfaces in area 0, while the second version specifically enables it just on the GigabitEthernet1/0/0 interface (ge-1/0/0.0 in JunOS) which is in area 0. The first option more closely matches the \"autoconfig\" behavior in Cisco.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface ge-1/0/0.0 area 0'}]]": 1,
